<worksheet xml:id="e-activity">
<title>M220 Module 8: Eigentheory</title>

<author>
<personnam>Dr. Anastasiia Minenkova</personnam>
</author>

<introduction>

<p>
Name: _______________________
</p>

<p>
Problem Number: _______________________
</p>

</introduction>

<subsection>
<title>Learning Outcomes</title>
<list>
<dl>
      <li>
        <title>E1</title>

        <p>
          Determine whether a vector is an eigenvector of a given matrix.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>E2</title>

        <p>
         Find the eigenvalue associated to an eigenvector.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>E3</title>

        <p>
          Use the characteristic polynomial to find the eigenvalues of a given matrix.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>E4</title>

        <p>
        find the algebraic and geometric multiplicities of eigenvalues. </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>E5</title>

        <p>
          find a basis for the eigenspace associated to an eigenvalue.
        </p>
      </li>
            <li>
        <title>E6</title>

        <p>
          determine whether a given matrix is diagonalizable.
        </p>
      </li>
            <li>
        <title>E7</title>

        <p>
          diagonalize a diagonalizable matrix.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
</list>
</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Worksheet Instructions</title>

<ol>
<li>
Find your chosen number and keep it throughout the semester.
</li>

<li>
Look at each section of the worksheet.
</li>

<li>
For each exercise group, complete the exercise matching your chosen
number.
</li>

<li>
Example: If your number is 5, complete Exercise 5 in every exercise
group.
</li>

<li>
Show your work clearly and check your answers.
</li>

<li>
Refer to the presentation instructions after completing the worksheet.
</li>
</ol>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="Eassignments">

<title>Assignments</title>
</subsection>
    <dl>

      <li>
        <title>E1</title>
<p>
    determine whether <m>x</m> and <m>v</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.
</p>
</li><li><title>E2</title><p>
    check if <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue associated to an eigenvector
    (either <m>x</m> or <m>v</m> ), if not what is the actual eigenvalue?
</p></li><li><title>E3-E7</title><p>
    diagonalize <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> if possible while addressing all the
    learning outcomes.
</p>
      </li>
          </dl>

